RISKS_INTRO = 'Situations the connector must handle explicitly. Most follow from three Horus traits: no documented idempotency key, entries that get a new id when updated, and lists without documented pagination.'

EDGE_CASES = [
    {'case': 'A create call times out or returns 500 (client, invoice, operation, account).', 'handling': 'Never retry blindly. If the record has a key (VAT number or external reference for clients, account number for accounts, document number for invoices and operations), search for it first and re-post only if it is not found. Without a key (an invoice or operation sent without a number, a client without VAT number or external reference), there is no automatic retry: the connector returns an “outcome unknown” error that says what to check. Before that, a supplier invoice is looked up by supplier, date and total, and an operation numbered by Horus by date, accounts and amounts; both can miss or mismatch under concurrent posting. An unconfirmed operation pushed through the API can still be deleted if it turns out to be a duplicate; invoices cannot. Identical calls in flight are processed one after the other (proposal). This is best-effort: Horus does not document whether a timed-out request can still be committed, or whether a new entry is listed at once (H3). Consumers that need safe retries should send a number (the Chift contract recommends invoice_number for idempotency).', 'routes': 'All write routes', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'Creation succeeds but the read-back that builds the response fails (timeout, 404 or 500).', 'handling': 'Never re-create and never return an error for that request: the record exists, and companies, accounts, invoices and confirmed operations cannot be deleted through the API. Return the new id with the values the connector wrote, mapped as a later read would return them (number empty when Horus generated it), log the failure and retry the read-back later. A folder mismatch on an id just created is logged as an alert, not returned as an error.', 'routes': 'All write routes', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'Attaching the PDF fails after the entry was created.', 'handling': 'Check whether the entry already has a document before retrying the attachment; never re-create the entry.', 'routes': 'Invoice creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'The client’s VAT number already exists, as a customer or as a supplier only.', 'handling': 'Pre-check before creating. Return 400 ERROR_PARTNER_ALREADY_EXISTS with the existing id (every match when several companies share the number), and suggest <code>force_merge</code> when the match is a supplier. How Horus stores and compares VAT numbers is undocumented (H25).', 'routes': 'Client creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'The invoice date falls in a period the accountant has closed.', 'handling': 'Check the fiscal-year closure dates before posting, as Horus recommends, reading them fresh at each write, and return a clear error. The invoice request has no period field, and the documentation does not say whether Horus itself refuses a posting into a closed period (question H6).', 'routes': 'Invoice and operation creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'A request with status “posted” on a connection in review mode.', 'handling': 'Pushed unconfirmed. The response returns status “draft”, and reads return the entry as a draft until the accountant confirms it in Horus (outstanding items only when unposted entries are requested; invoice retrieval per C10). Proposal, C1.', 'routes': 'Invoice and operation creation, invoice retrieval, outstanding items', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'An invoice to be confirmed (direct mode) without invoice_number.', 'handling': 'Proposed default: pushed unconfirmed for review, without a number, and returned as “draft”. A confirmed sales invoice needs a number, Horus offers no automatic invoice numbering through the API, and what it stores for a confirmed purchase without a number is undocumented. Alternatives: reject with 422, or generate the next number per journal as an opt-in with a lock per journal, since concurrent pushes or entries keyed in Horus can take the same number (C7, H3).', 'routes': 'Invoice creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'Header totals and line totals differ by rounding.', 'handling': 'Horus checks that totals equal base plus tax and that the lines add up to the header, and by default recalculates each line’s tax from base × rate, adapting a sent amount within one cent (documented). The connector checks first: a difference within a rounding tolerance agreed with Chift becomes a correction line when the consumer supplies <code>invoice_correction</code>; a larger difference, or one without invoice_correction, is rejected with 422. VAT is booked as sent (OverrideTaxAmounts = true, proposal, D16), with an optional check against untaxed amount × rate (C2, C19).', 'routes': 'Invoice creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'A refund sent with negative amounts, a document with mixed-sign lines, or an invoice with a negative total.', 'handling': 'Refunds are sent to Horus with positive amounts and the credit-note type: a negative refund is multiplied by −1 as a whole. Mixed-sign documents and invoices with a negative total are rejected until Chift and Horus confirm otherwise (C5, H2).', 'routes': 'Invoice creation', 'label': 'A'},
    {'case': 'An analytic split such as 33 / 33 / 34 %.', 'handling': 'Largest-remainder rounding (ties broken by plan order, then account id) so cost lines sum exactly to the line base amount; combinations that round to 0.00 are dropped.', 'routes': 'Invoice creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'A tax_code that is not a valid Horus combination for the folder’s legislation (Belgium or Luxembourg) and the invoice direction.', 'handling': 'Reject with 422 before calling Horus. For a folder that is not VAT liable, the combination to send is undocumented (H1).', 'routes': 'Invoice creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'A foreign-currency invoice or operation.', 'handling': 'Invoices: allowed when the folder accepts currencies (documented). Miscellaneous operations: Horus stores them in EUR (no currency field), so a foreign-currency operation is converted at the supplied rate, as Chift’s contract describes for journals that do not allow other currencies; the rate is required and the original currency is not kept (proposal; who converts, C15). On read, ledger amounts are treated as EUR until H4 is answered, and invoice-currency amounts exist only for open sales invoices: such an invoice is returned in its own currency while open and in EUR once paid, and purchase invoices always in EUR; EUR amounts are never paired with a foreign currency code (assumption, D4). On outstanding items, the rate returned is derived from the two amounts returned, and one partner’s items may mix currencies. One stable EUR representation instead, until H4 is answered, is a Chift choice (C16).', 'routes': 'Invoices, operations, outstanding items', 'label': 'A'},
    {'case': 'The accountant edits an invoice created through the API.', 'handling': 'Updated invoices and operations get a new book entry id (documented for updates; whether edits in Horus Office behave the same is H10), so the Chift id changes. Consumers reconcile on journal and document number, as Horus recommends, and treat an id that disappears as replaced or deleted. An entry pushed without a number (possible while it awaits accountant review) has no such key and cannot be found again after an edit. The proposal is to require a number on push, or to let the connector assign one (questions C7, C10, H10).', 'routes': 'Invoice and operation creation, invoice retrieval, outstanding items', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'A credit note stored in a sales or purchase daybook.', 'handling': 'Both daybook types are read. Proposed rule: the daybook type decides; only in folders without a credit-note daybook (where the connector itself posts refunds into the sales or purchase daybook) is the entry classified by the sign of the customer or supplier line. The type on open sales items is a consistency check, so an invoice keeps its class once paid. Known limitation: a credit note keyed into a sales daybook of a folder that has a credit-note daybook is returned as an invoice with negative amounts (questions H2, C5).', 'routes': 'Invoice retrieval', 'label': 'A'},
    {'case': 'An invoice with a negative total (credit direction) read from Horus.', 'handling': 'Returned as an invoice with negative amounts, except in folders without a credit-note daybook, where it is classified as a refund (question C5).', 'routes': 'Invoice retrieval', 'label': 'A'},
    {'case': 'Several possible journals and no journal_id.', 'handling': 'Auto-select by daybook type when exactly one candidate exists; otherwise 400 “journal_id required”.', 'routes': 'Invoice and operation creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'An inactive account or cost section, analytics sent to a folder without the analytics module, or analytics in a folder with a mandatory free-type dimension.', 'handling': 'Reject with 422 before calling Horus (for mandatory free-type dimensions, a v1 proposal, D8).', 'routes': 'Write routes', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'A partner is created from partner_info, then Horus rejects the invoice.', 'handling': 'All connector checks run before the partner is written. If Horus still rejects the invoice, the new company (or the role added under the opt-in of D10) stays, since there is no delete endpoint; its id is returned in the error so the consumer can reuse it.', 'routes': 'Invoice creation', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'An id that belongs to another folder of the same license.', 'handling': 'Check the folder on every call by id; return 400.', 'routes': 'All', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'Partial payments and instalments.', 'handling': 'Clients: the open amount is Horus’s own (open sales invoices list). Suppliers: Horus gives ledger lines only, so the open amount is the amount of each unmatched line; only if Horus also returns partially matched lines is it netted per matching group, with the remainder going to the invoice lines oldest due date first (assumption, question H8). Whether partially paid supplier invoices are returned at all is the key open point: a fallback read of the supplier ledger and a check against Horus’s own totals are planned. Horus has no instalment entity: each customer or supplier line has one due date, so an invoice with several such lines (possible for entries keyed in Horus Office) appears as one item per line and is never counted twice (C17).', 'routes': 'Outstanding items, invoice retrieval', 'label': 'A'},
    {'case': 'An open item carried over from the previous fiscal year.', 'handling': 'Identify the opening entry through its entry type and link the original document where Horus provides it; otherwise leave <code>original_document</code> empty. If Horus returns both the original line and its carry-forward line, the connector keeps one of them (matched on company, amount and fiscal year) and logs the pair (proposal, H8).', 'routes': 'Outstanding items', 'label': 'A'},
    {'case': 'Two Chift calls for the same consumer refresh the token at the same time.', 'handling': 'Serialise refreshes per connection; refresh tokens are single-use.', 'routes': 'All', 'label': 'F'},
    {'case': 'A token refresh times out or fails with a server error.', 'handling': 'Retry the refresh with the same refresh token, with backoff, before marking the connection as disconnected; only a rejection by the token endpoint ends the connection. Whether a refresh whose response was lost has already used up the token is undocumented (H24).', 'routes': 'All', 'label': 'A'},
    {'case': 'A Chift consumer disconnects or is deleted.', 'handling': 'Delete the stored API URL and tokens at once and log the event without tokens. Horus documents no revoke endpoint, and refresh tokens do not expire on their own: the user revokes the grant by uninstalling the integration in Horus.', 'routes': 'All', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'Several Chift consumers connect the same Horus license, or the authorising user leaves the firm.', 'handling': 'Store the license and user ids at activation to detect a license that is already connected. The effect of repeated grants, and the fate of the tokens of a deactivated user, are undocumented (H24, C12).', 'routes': 'All', 'label': '?'},
    {'case': 'Horus returns an enum value the connector does not know (for example a new daybook type).', 'handling': 'Map it to Chift “unknown” or a safe default and log it; unknown properties are ignored.', 'routes': 'All', 'label': 'D'},
    {'case': 'A large folder (an accounting firm’s client with years of entries).', 'handling': 'Full lists with emulated pagination, narrow month windows, per-folder caching and gzip. Incremental reads (updated_after) filter entry headers natively but must re-read the ledger lines of the periods concerned, so their cost grows with the look-back window (C11). Horus documents no size, rate or time limits: expected to work for typical folder sizes; behaviour on large folders is unverified (H5). A volume test on the largest test folder is the exit criterion of phase 1; if full-period reads do not scale, a mandatory date window or journal filter on invoice retrieval is the fallback (proposal).', 'routes': 'All list routes', 'label': '?'},
]

from curated_questions import *  # noqa: E402,F401,F403  (questions for Horus and Chift)
